              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bearings and slats</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stainless steel track bearings; 0.75 in rubber tank treads</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Article agrees on bearings</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Distance, speed, time, calories, total miles</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Manual: battery powered, wakes when belt moves</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Home warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 yr frame, 5 yr parts, 2 yr console, 1 yr labour</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Manual: 15/7/2/1 in one table; 5 yr frame, 2 yr parts in its warranty text</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Three weight ratings from one seller</h3>
          <p>
            The capacity conflict is the one that matters. The current product page says 400 lb. The owner's
            manual, in both its safety notes and its specification table, says 475 lb. The seller's own
            article comparing the CT700 with the AssaultRunner says 350 lb. The manual on the seller's site
            dates from the machine's launch, and the product page is the most recently updated, so 400 lb is
            probably the seller's current figure, but we cannot confirm which applies to the unit you would
            receive. If you are heavier than about 300 lb, or buying for a studio where you do not control who
            uses it, ask the seller to confirm the rating in writing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains how much margin to leave.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A long deck and a tall step</h3>
          <p>
            A 66.6-inch running surface — 66 inches in the manual — is long for a curved treadmill; the
            AssaultRunner Pro lists 62. Extra length gives a tall runner room to move forward and back on the
            curve to change pace, which is how you control a self-powered belt. The 19-inch width is two inches
            wider than the AssaultRunner and one inch narrower than the CT550. The cost of that size is height
            and bulk: a 13.5-inch step-up, the tallest in SB Fitness's range, a 78-inch length and a 308 lb
            machine. Check your ceiling height with the step-up added to your own height and stride, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space guide</a>
            covers the clearances around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ct700-vs-ct550" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. SB Fitness CT700 vs CT550: What the Extra $600 Buys</h2>
<p>
            The CT550 is the CT700's nearest sibling and the obvious alternative. We reviewed it from the same
            seller's listings in our
            <a href="/sb-fitness-ct550-curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SB Fitness CT550 review</a>.
            On the seller's product pages, the two compare like this:
